WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
- Batteries may be small,

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.990
but for the United Auto Workers
Union, they're a big deal.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:07.170
- It's gonna change
the future of our union

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.940
and the future of our industry.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.163
- At least 25,000 members of the UAW

00:00:11.163 --> 00:00:13.320
have been striking simultaneously

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.200
at plants owned by GM,
Ford, and Stellantis,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.012
as they demand increased
pay and better job security.

00:00:19.012 --> 00:00:21.060
(strikers chanting)

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.620
And at the heart of their negotiations

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.720
lies a question around how the UAW

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.290
will fit into automakers plans
to build more and more EVs.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.330
And whether the workers who make these

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.560
will become unionized.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.750
- This union is really
fighting over positions

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.370
that aren't made yet.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.890
- So what do America's
EV and battery ambitions

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.020
mean for the future of the UAW?

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.055
Let's find out.

00:00:41.055 --> 00:00:42.013
(upbeat music)

00:00:42.013 --> 00:00:45.900
(Strikers chanting Auto Workers Union)

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:46.950
So this is the first time

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.230
in the UAW's almost 90 year history

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.510
that the group has gone on strike

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:53.190
at all three automakers at the same time.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:55.020
Part of the UAW's demands have been

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.610
for cost of living adjustments,

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.830
the right to strike over plant closures,

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:02.190
and a 40% pay rise over
the next four years.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.050
And negotiations have been tricky.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.940
- So I'll tell you what I'm
gonna do with their proposal.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.520
I'm gonna file it in its proper place,

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:10.320
'cause that's where it belongs, the trash.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.760
- A lot of the tension has been around

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.730
where the money for that 40%
pay rise could come from.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:15.960
And to help explain why,

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.480
here's "Wsjs Autos" reporter Nora Eckert.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.520
- Okay, webcam, sorry George.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.710
I might need to get warmed
up a little bit here.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.720
This is coming at a time
where the automakers

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.290
are really focused on keeping
costs low where they can,

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:32.100
so that they can fund the
transition to producing EVs.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.410
- [George] Part of that expense
is because the raw materials

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:38.400
needed to make Evs, can
cost up to 125% more

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.500
than those used in
internal combustion engine

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.670
or ICE vehicles.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.080
- But the union has said

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.630
that the companies can
afford their demands

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:48.300
even as they're funneling
billions of dollars

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:49.950
into this EV transition.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.530
- When the big three say
the future's uncertain

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.780
and that the EV transition's expensive,

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.850
remember that they've made
a quarter of a trillion

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.940
in North American profits
over the last decade.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:01.830
- But when it comes to EV manufacturing,

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:05.580
the bigger concern for the
UAW is around job security.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.130
See, the transition from making
gas cars to electric ones

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:11.340
is dramatically reshaping how
many auto workers are needed,

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:12.900
and what those roles are.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.900
While tasks like installing
interior trim or fitting seats

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.120
are unlikely to change
much in the transition,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.670
roles that center around
building the combustion engine

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:21.660
are at risk.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.070
According to congressional research,

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:26.310
that could mean nearly
150,000 jobs are exposed.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:29.970
And even Ford's own CEO, Jim
Farley once told a conference

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:33.630
the EVs would require 40%
less labor than ICE vehicles.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.730
- But the automakers have assured workers

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:39.000
that as many of those jobs will
be transitioned as possible,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.010
so they'll be moving folks over

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.140
from these ICE plants to EV plants.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:44.580
- While jobs on combustion engine

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.170
and transmission production lines

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.030
are likely to be phased out,

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.460
new jobs in new battery
manufacturing facilities

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:51.450
will spring up.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:55.050
But that could still present
a major issue for the UAW.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:58.260
- The plan was to draw down
engine and transmission plants

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.400
and permanently replace them
with low wage battery jobs.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.990
- See, most of the new battery factories

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:07.740
aren't 100% owned and
operated by automakers,

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.270
they're joint ventures.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:12.300
GM has a factory with LG
and plans for two more.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.910
Ford has plans for several with SK On,

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:17.880
and Stellantis has teamed up
with Samsung for its factories.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.680
And the problem for the UAW

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.173
is that these factories aren't
automatically unionized.

00:03:22.173 --> 00:03:23.760
- And the union is fighting

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.010
for the security of those future jobs,

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:27.330
which is really interesting.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.370
- [George] But that
appears to be changing.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:31.770
In December, 2022, the workers at LG

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.740
and GM's battery facility
voted to unionize,

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.510
and fast forward to the present day.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.720
- GM has now agreed, in writing

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.500
to place their electric
battery manufacturing

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.690
under our National Master Agreement.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.120
What this will mean for our membership

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.010
cannot be understated.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:50.843
- The question now

00:03:50.843 --> 00:03:52.920
is whether GM's agreement
will set the stage

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.980
for other automakers battery
facilities to follow suit.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:56.880
If that happens,

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.160
it could drive costs up
for these automakers.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.740
The average hourly labor
costs for a unionized OEM

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.340
is around $65 an hour,
according to industry data.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.650
Whereas Tesla, which
currently isn't unionized,

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:11.280
has hourly labor costs
of around $45 an hour.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.730
And so if these automakers
pay their workers union rates

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:16.230
at their battery facilities,

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.690
it could make them less cost competitive

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.190
compared to the likes of Tesla

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:22.560
or foreign manufacturers
like those in Asia.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.450
- Throughout this whole
bargaining process,

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:27.397
the automakers have sort
of repeated this mantra of,

00:04:27.397 --> 00:04:29.520
"We cannot be competitive companies

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:31.590
if our labor costs inflate

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:33.990
as much as the union's
demands would make them."

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:34.980
- But if automakers

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.470
don't come to an agreement with the UAW,

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.990
they may struggle to
make vehicles altogether.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:42.660
- I've heard members who
wanna bring down the hammer,

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.550
strike all the truck plants,

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.470
hit the big three where it hurts.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:49.260
And there is a time and place
for that, and believe me,

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:52.230
if the big three don't
continue to make progress,

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:54.150
that time's gonna be coming real soon.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.550
- So even though the strikes
have gone on for weeks,

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:58.590
this could just be the very beginning

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:01.042
of a long road ahead for the UAW.

00:05:01.042 --> 00:05:03.792
(dramatic music)

